text	Three points in the letter, three open issues (#12, #13, #14). One point conflicts with an issue, one matches, and one is only partly covered.  **1. Which customers a new rate applies to: conflict with #12** - **Letter:** a new rate applies only to customers the partner brings after the change. Current customers keep their commissions as they are. - **#12:** the merchant chooses in a dialog. The default is **All customers**, so current customers pay the new rate from their next payment. "New customers only" is just the other option. - So the issue's default is the opposite of what the client asked for. Its acceptance test checks that the 3 current customers move to 7% when the merchant keeps the default. Under the client's rule they stay at 5%. - #12 also says the dialog means "the client no longer has to answer our question 1 of 23 Oct". The decision behind that was made on 29 Oct. The client answered the next day, so that reason no longer holds.  **2. Payouts: matches #13** Both say monthly, on the 5th. No change needed.  **3. Rate history: only partly covered by #12** - **Letter:** every change of a partner's rate shows in the partner's history. - **#12:** the history records the choice made in the dialog. The dialog only appears for a partner who already has customers. Nothing covers a rate change for a partner with no customers, and no issue is about rate history on its own.  **#14 (CSV export)** is not in the letter. Nothing conflicts with it.  **Decision for you:** someone has to choose between the client's rule and the 29 Oct decision. - **Follow the client:** #12 becomes "new rate applies to new customers only". Then either drop the dialog or make "New customers only" the default, and rewrite the acceptance test. - **Keep the dialog:** the client needs a reply explaining why current customers would move to the new rate by default.  Either way, someone should file a separate issue to record every rate change in the partner's history.  I have not edited or filed anything on GitHub. Tell me which way to go and I will update #12 and file the history issue.
turn	2
text	Here is a draft. It follows the option you are leaning to: the new rate applies only to new customers by default.  > **Change of a partner's rate.** Thank you for your answer. We will build it as you describe. When you change the rate of a partner, the new rate applies only to the customers that the partner brings after the change. The commissions on the partner's current customers stay as they are. Each change of the rate will show in the partner's history, with its date, the old rate and the new rate. We can also add an option to apply a new rate to the current customers too, in the cases where you want that. The option would be off by default. Tell us if it would be useful to you.  Notes for the team meeting: - The last two sentences keep the dialog from #12 as an option. If the team drops the dialog, delete them. The paragraph still works without them. - "With its date, the old rate and the new rate" is my guess at what "history" means. The client did not say this, so check it before you send. - This is not a decision yet, so I have not changed #12 or filed anything. After the meeting, tell me the outcome. I will then update #12 (its default, its reason and its acceptance test) and file the issue for the rate history.
